-- title:
--   Geometric section-cost comparison with a degree-4n fibre spectrum
-- statement:
--   Prove the same uniform section-dimension comparison W_min*E_min<=C*dim(V_mn) under the original A.1 analytic hypotheses, with the following additional algebraic data available: a nonzero polynomial H of degree at most 4n, a distinct root set of cardinality at most 4n, and proof that every whole-fibre anchor outside the lattice has wp-value in that root set.
--
--   The constant is chosen before m,n,U,X,Q and H; it must be uniform in all of them. The cap function, its correctness hypothesis, the exact finite fibre-coordinate list Z, and the definitions of the minimum anchor weight and minimum chart cost are unchanged.
--
--   The complete spectrum theorem constructs this certificate from the original hypotheses. Conversely, the previous comparison implies this one by ignoring the certificate, with the same C. The remaining work is still the global geometric multiplicity/degree estimate, now with a finite, degree-bounded set of regular elliptic fibre values available. This statement does not turn the spectrum bound into a bound for Z.card, treat lattice anchors, or solve the line-anchor branch.
-- source:
--   Derived algebraic fibre bound for Senthil Kumar K (2026), Appendix A.2, https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/proceedings-of-the-edinburgh-mathematical-society/article/algebraic-independence-of-values-of-weierstrass-elliptic-and-zeta-functions/E91A8EEEB1F63536D95D1DE7D7DD47E2#app1. The degree-4n bound is derived here, not quoted from the paper. Fix a nonzero regular slice, retain the Weierstrass cubic, and reduce to A(x)+y*B(x), with deg A<=2n and deg B<=2n-2. The norm A(x)^2-(4*x^3-g2*x-g3)*B(x)^2 is nonzero by degree parity and has degree at most 4n. Every whole-fibre anchor off the lattice projects to a root. This replaces qualitative compact-region finiteness by a uniform bound on distinct wp-values, not on the coordinates b themselves. The global geometric cost estimate is still required, now with this annihilator certificate available; the reduction preserves C exactly.

